00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:09.770
Phil: Most often tends to be a
noise - either hypersensitive or

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:15.980
hyposensitive . I used to support a young
man, and he would have ear defenders

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:22.130
because we had the impression he was,
and he was, fairly hypersensitive.

00:00:22.425 --> 00:00:25.824
Let me get this right way
around, hypersensitive to noise.

00:00:26.655 --> 00:00:29.865
So used the ear defenders for that, but
then would make a lot of noise himself.

00:00:29.895 --> 00:00:35.635
Very often, he would be the loudest
in the room, but the difference there

00:00:35.635 --> 00:00:40.185
is, I suppose a logic there is, he
was, you had the air defenders, not

00:00:40.185 --> 00:00:45.735
only to sort of lessen the sound,
the impact of the sound in his

00:00:45.995 --> 00:00:50.595
environment, but also for the physical
sensory input of the pressure as well.

00:00:52.065 --> 00:00:56.654
And in terms of him being the
loudest that's a sound, that's

00:00:56.654 --> 00:01:01.004
a noise he can control, he can
control his own noise level.

00:01:01.015 --> 00:01:05.265
So there was less anxiety on that part,
but then was still hypersensitive to

00:01:06.465 --> 00:01:08.715
the noise of others in his environment.

00:01:09.035 --> 00:01:15.660
Kerry: There's a hypersensitivity to
sound for two learners in my classroom,

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:21.570
and there's the use of air defenders
and noise thermometers, and we've got

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:25.440
strategies put in place for what we
can do if the classroom's too loud,

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:29.640
but on the flip side of that, they
themselves quite enjoy making loud sounds.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.940
It's when it's an unexpected
sound that we're not too keen.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.160
And we find that just
a little bit too much.

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:40.635
We make sure that our classroom is as
much as possible a quiet environment.

00:01:40.635 --> 00:01:43.755
So there's no unnecessary
noises being made.

00:01:43.755 --> 00:01:47.005
So, you know, the staff team aren't
chatting to each other about what they

00:01:47.005 --> 00:01:49.995
ate for tea last night or what they did at
the weekend, because that's unnecessary.

00:01:50.994 --> 00:01:52.965
So it's trying to make sure
that the environment is as

00:01:52.965 --> 00:01:53.925
accomodating as possible.

00:01:54.575 --> 00:01:58.985
Zoe T: Most of the pupils that
our school will have hypo or

00:01:58.985 --> 00:02:01.445
hyper sensory sensitivities, yeah.

00:02:02.345 --> 00:02:06.990
Well usually what happens is that
that person becomes, if they're

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:11.670
hypersensitive to something like
hyperacusis, for example, the person

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:18.630
would become emotionally overwhelmed
and need to withdraw or would have

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:24.780
some kind of potentially an emotional
outburst or some people may get

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:26.850
violent in those kinds of situations.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:34.080
And people who are hyposensitive, that can
be difficult because you don't necessarily

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:37.575
know what's kind of going on for them.

00:02:38.505 --> 00:02:44.985
So yes, those sorts of things can
be a major obstacle to learning, to

00:02:45.195 --> 00:02:50.265
communicating, to interacting and need to
be really well understood by the people

00:02:50.265 --> 00:02:54.615
that are supporting the autistic person
who's experiencing any of those in order

00:02:54.615 --> 00:02:59.565
for that person to be able to participate
in whatever it is they're trying to do.
